course: dictionary
lesson: 13
title: Abolish: ending something for good
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When a word is new, look at the words around it for clues. Abolish is a verb, an action word, so the clues show what it does. «In 1888 Brazil was the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery.» The sentence tells you that slavery was still there until people decided to end it. So to abolish means to end something officially, so that it no longer exists.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Brazil was the last country in the Americas to end slavery officially.» It still makes sense, so your meaning fits. Abolish is stronger than just end: something is ended by people in power, through a law or a decision.
explain: Look at the other examples. «Serfdom was abolished in 1861» tells you that a rule and a way of life were ended by a decision. Abolish is used with things like a law, a tax, or a custom. When a thing is abolished, it is not just forgotten; someone decided to get rid of it.
fact: Abolish means to officially end a law, rule, system or custom so that it no longer exists.
fact: Abolish can also mean to get rid of something completely.
fact: «Abolished» is the past tense, and «abolishing» is the -ing form.
fact: «Serfdom was abolished in 1861» in Russia, so people in power made a decision to end it.
fact: Abolish is often used in history lessons, when countries or leaders decide to end something unfair or old.
exercise: «Slavery was abolished in the United States in 1865.» What does abolished mean here? | Made much bigger | Moved to another place | *Officially ended by a decision | Forgotten by everyone by accident
exercise: Ella says abolish means forget. What is the better answer? | It means to build something new | *It means officially ended by a decision, not just forgotten | It means to count money carefully | It means to shout very loudly
exercise: Find the verb from abolish in this sentence: «The club is abolishing its old rules.» | club | rules | old | *abolishing
test: «Brazil was the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery.» Which phrase means the same as abolish here? | carry on with | copy quietly | *officially end | admire
test: Which word is the past tense of abolish? | *abolished | abolishes | abolish | abolishing
exam: Which sentence uses abolish correctly? | *Parliament voted to abolish the old tax on tea | The river will abolish the hills by Friday | We abolish our lunch every single day | The cat decided to abolish its tail
